  "TourPoints": [
    {
      "Id": "cffb54db-aef9-4b7f-84f5-fb1be3b5fa6a",
      "Description": "In computer graphics, positions are defined using three values along the x, y, and z axes.\nIn TiXL's coordinate system, x points to the right, y points upward, and z points toward the camera.\nYou can use the thumb, index finger, and middle finger of your right hand to remember their orientation."
    },
    {
      "Id": "f5a80dc7-6fbf-471c-a307-7b432fd8de29",
      "Description": "Other software may use different coordinate systems.\nFor example, some CAD applications use x and y as the ground plane.\nWith TiXL's default camera, the position (0, 0, 0) is centered on the screen, so an object that is 2 units tall touches both the top and bottom edges of the view."
    },
    {
      "Id": "a0827bd4-5fa4-4150-9071-0f3a7437238d",
      "Description": "We can use [AnimValue] and [Vector3] to build a simple animation.\nBoth operators will be covered in more detail later."
    },
    {
      "Id": "5b018788-1ab7-4760-8af5-8d95696c1509",
      "Description": "Connect the operator to the [Vector3] to create the reference animation.",
      "Style": "CallToAction"
    }
  ]
}